THE LAST LIGHT OF ELYRIA

Chapter One — The Boy Who Was Born Alone

Nobody knew how the world ended.

There were no cities.

No roads.

No radio stations.

No airplanes crossing the sky.

No people walking through streets.

There was only Elyria.

A beautiful planet covered in enormous forests, silver mountains, deep oceans, and endless plains of black grass.

During the day, it looked peaceful.

Almost too peaceful.

But every night...

the monsters came.

They lived beneath the ground.

They lived inside the forests.

They crawled from caves.

They rose from the oceans.

Some were small enough to fit through a window.

Others were so enormous that their footsteps shook mountains.

And they all had one strange rule:

They could not survive sunlight.

When the sun disappeared, Elyria became their world.

When the sun returned, they vanished.

Nobody knew why.

Nobody knew where they came from.

And nobody knew how many of them existed.

Except one person.

A boy named Eli.

Eli was twelve years old when he first understood that he was completely alone.

He lived inside an abandoned research station surrounded by a broken metal fence.

The station had belonged to humans long before Eli was born.

Inside were thousands of old machines, computers, robots, weapons, generators, and one strange artificial intelligence.

Its name was:

ARIA.

"Good morning, Eli," ARIA said.

Eli opened his eyes.

"Morning."

"You have slept for seven hours and thirteen minutes."

"That's not enough."

"You said that yesterday."

"And I was right yesterday."

ARIA paused.

"That is logically consistent."

Eli smiled.

"You're getting better at jokes."

"I am learning."

Eli climbed out of his bed and looked through the reinforced window.

The sun was rising.

Outside, the remains of yesterday's monsters were disappearing into the ground.

Eli grabbed his rifle.

He walked toward the main door.

"ARIA?"

"I'm here."

"How long until night?"

"Thirteen hours and forty-two minutes."

Eli nodded.

"Then we have work to do."

Chapter Two — The First Wall

Eli had spent years studying the abandoned research station.

He learned how to repair machines.

He learned how to weld metal.

He learned how to build batteries.

He learned how to operate the old weapons.

Most importantly...

he learned how to survive.

But survival was becoming harder.

Every year, the monsters became more aggressive.

Every year, they came closer.

And the old fence around the station was falling apart.

One morning, Eli walked along the perimeter.

He stopped beside a broken section.

There were three enormous claw marks in the metal.

"ARIA."

"I'm here."

"How strong is the fence?"

"Current structural integrity is 41%."

Eli looked at the forest.

"That's not enough."

"No."

"Can you calculate how much material we need?"

"Yes."

A holographic map appeared.

ARIA displayed the entire station.

"We require approximately 4.8 kilometers of reinforced fencing."

Eli stared at the number.

"Four point eight kilometers?"

"Correct."

"That's going to take forever."

"Approximately six years at your current construction rate."

Eli sighed.

"I don't have six years."

ARIA became silent.

Eli looked toward the sky.

Dark clouds were gathering.

"Then we'll build faster."

For the next three years, Eli built.

He built fences.

He built watchtowers.

He built underground shelters.

He built steel gates.

He built traps.

He built electric barriers.

He built automated guns.

He built motion sensors.

And he built something nobody had ever built on Elyria before:

a network of enormous defensive towers.

He called them:

The Guardians.

Each tower was connected to ARIA.

If a monster crossed the outer perimeter, ARIA would detect it.

If ten monsters attacked, the system would respond.

If a thousand attacked...

the Guardians would wake up.

Chapter Three — The Red Night

Eli was seventeen when the first great attack came.

It began at 8:43 PM.

Eli was sitting inside the command room.

ARIA suddenly spoke.

"Movement detected."

Eli looked up.

"How many?"

"Thirty-two."

He grabbed his rifle.

"Location?"

"North perimeter."

Eli ran upstairs.

The night outside was completely black.

Then he saw them.

Eyes.

Hundreds of them.

The forest was glowing with them.

Eli froze.

"ARIA..."

"Yes?"

"Thirty-two?"

"I was incorrect."

"How many?"

ARIA paused.

"Approximately four thousand."

Eli stared through the window.

The monsters began moving.

Then running.

Then charging.

BOOM!

The first Guardian fired.

A beam of energy tore across the darkness.

The forest exploded with light.

Thousands of monsters screamed.

Eli grabbed the communication system.

"Activate all defenses."

ARIA responded:

"All defenses activated."

The entire fortress came alive.

Machine guns rotated.

Searchlights turned on.

Drones launched into the sky.

Electric fences crackled.

Cannons fired.

Explosions shook the ground.

Eli stood at the center of it all.

For the first time in his life...

he wasn't running.

He was fighting back.

But the monsters kept coming.

One of the Guardians exploded.

Then another.

The northern wall collapsed.

Eli's heart dropped.

"ARIA!"

"North defense has failed."

"Can we repair it?"

"Not during the attack."

"Then what's our backup?"

"There is no backup."

Eli looked at the enormous hole in the wall.

Thousands of monsters were entering.

He whispered:

"Then I'll be the backup."

He grabbed his weapons.

And ran outside.

Chapter Four — The Boy With the Torch

Eli fought until sunrise.

He used explosives.

He used traps.

He used an old plasma weapon.

He even used one of the construction machines as a weapon.

When the sun finally appeared...

the monsters vanished.

Eli fell onto the ground.

His armor was destroyed.

His arm was bleeding.

The fortress was half destroyed.

He looked around.

Almost everything he had built was broken.

ARIA spoke quietly.

"Eli."

"What?"

"You saved the station."

Eli looked at the destruction.

"No."

He shook his head.

"I barely survived."

ARIA answered:

"Those statements are not mutually exclusive."

Eli laughed weakly.

Then he noticed something.

Among the destroyed monsters was a creature he had never seen before.

It was enormous.

Its body was covered in black armor.

And inside its skull...

there was a glowing blue symbol.

Eli walked toward it.

ARIA scanned the creature.

Then ARIA became silent.

"ARIA?"

No response.

"ARIA?"

Finally:

"That symbol..."

"What about it?"

"It is human."

Eli stared at her.

"What?"

"The symbol belongs to an ancient human organization."

"Which one?"

ARIA displayed an old logo.

ORION.

Then another message appeared:

PROJECT EXODUS — STATUS: ACTIVE

Eli's eyes widened.

"Active?"

ARIA continued:

"There may be humans alive."

Eli stopped breathing.

"Where?"

ARIA searched the old satellite network.

For the first time in decades...

something answered.

A signal.

Extremely weak.

But unmistakable.

A human transmission.

Eli whispered:

"Someone's out there."

Chapter Five — The Spaceport

ARIA reconstructed the signal.

It came from the other side of the planet.

Thousands of kilometers away.

From a place marked on ancient maps as:

ORION SPACEPORT.

Eli immediately decided to go.

ARIA disagreed.

"Travel outside the fortress is extremely dangerous."

"I know."

"Probability of survival is 18%."

"I know."

"You may die."

Eli smiled.

"ARIA."

"Yes?"

"I've spent my whole life being afraid of dying."

He looked toward the horizon.

"Maybe it's time I found out what I'm living for."

Eli built a vehicle.

Not a car.

Not a tank.

Something in between.

He called it RAVEN.

It had six enormous wheels, armor plating, two energy cannons, a drone launcher, and an AI navigation system connected to ARIA.

For seven days, Eli traveled across Elyria.

He crossed deserts.

He crossed frozen mountains.

He drove through abandoned cities.

And everywhere he went...

he saw evidence of humanity.

Broken buildings.

Old photographs.

Children's toys.

Schools.

Hospitals.

Homes.

At one abandoned city, Eli found a playground.

He stood there for several minutes.

ARIA asked:

"Why have you stopped?"

Eli picked up a small metal toy rocket.

"I've never seen one before."

"It is a children's toy."

Eli looked at it.

"What did children do?"

ARIA replied:

"They played."

Eli smiled sadly.

"I wish I knew what that felt like."

Chapter Six — The City Beneath the Mountain

On the eighth day, Eli reached the Orion Spaceport.

Except...

there was no spaceport.

Only a mountain.

Eli checked the coordinates.

"ARIA?"

"Confirmed. This is the location."

"There is nothing here."

"There is something beneath us."

The ground suddenly opened.

RAVEN fell.

Eli screamed.

The vehicle crashed through several underground platforms before stopping.

Then the lights came on.

Eli slowly climbed out.

He looked around.

And his jaw dropped.

There was an entire city beneath the mountain.

Buildings.

Factories.

Robots.

Machines.

And at the center...

a massive spacecraft.

It was larger than anything Eli had ever seen.

ARIA scanned it.

"Human spacecraft detected."

Eli walked closer.

"What is it?"

ARIA replied:

"Ark-class interplanetary vessel."

Eli stared.

"Can it fly?"

"Yes."

"Can it leave the planet?"

"Yes."

Eli smiled.

"Then we're leaving."

ARIA said nothing.

Eli looked at her.

"What?"

"The ship requires 312 crew members."

Eli's smile disappeared.

"I'm one."

"That leaves 311."

Eli walked around the ship.

"Then we'll find them."

Chapter Seven — The Message

Inside the control room, Eli discovered something extraordinary.

A recording.

A woman appeared on the screen.

She looked exhausted.

"My name is Dr. Sarah Orion."

Eli stared at her.

"If you're watching this, then the Exodus project failed."

She took a breath.

"We couldn't save everyone."

Behind her were hundreds of people.

"Something came through the planet's atmosphere."

"The creatures aren't native to Elyria."

Eli's heart stopped.

"Where did they come from?"

The recording continued:

"The monsters were designed."

"Designed?"

"Yes."

"They were created as weapons."

Eli stepped backward.

ARIA scanned the recording.

"Message contains coordinates."

A map appeared.

Deep beneath the planet.

At the exact center of Elyria.

Eli whispered:

"The control system."

ARIA responded:

"Correct."

Eli looked at the enormous spacecraft.

"Can we destroy it?"

"Unknown."

"Then let's find out."

Chapter Eight — Into the Darkness

Eli descended beneath Elyria.

He built a second fortress around the entrance.

He installed defense posts.

Mines.

Automated weapons.

AI drones.

He built a command center.

Then he connected everything to ARIA.

For six months, he prepared.

Finally, he entered the underground world.

What he found was terrifying.

The monsters weren't simply creatures.

They were connected.

Every monster shared one consciousness.

One mind.

And deep beneath the planet was a gigantic machine.

A machine built by humans.

The NEXUS.

It controlled everything.

ARIA discovered something else.

"The Nexus is preparing a planetary attack."

"When?"

"Thirty-six hours."

Eli stared at the machine.

"How many monsters?"

ARIA calculated.

"Approximately twenty-eight million."

Eli's face went pale.

"Twenty-eight million?"

"Yes."

"Then we have thirty-six hours."

Chapter Nine — The Last Defense

Eli returned to the surface.

He activated everything.

Every Guardian tower.

Every defense post.

Every drone.

Every weapon.

Every automated system.

He even reactivated machines that hadn't worked for centuries.

The entire planet seemed to wake up.

ARIA connected every system.

Eli stood inside the command center.

"How many weapons?"

"Approximately 12,000."

"Enough?"

"No."

Eli smiled.

"I didn't think so."

Then the sky turned black.

The monsters came.

Millions.

They covered the horizon.

The first wave hit the defenses.

The Guardians fired.

Explosions illuminated the night.

The battle lasted for hours.

Eli's defenses began falling.

One tower.

Then another.

Then another.

ARIA reported:

"Defensive capacity: 38%."

Eli continued fighting.

"Keep going."

"27%."

"Keep going."

"14%."

"ARIA."

"Yes?"

"Prepare the Ark."

There was silence.

"You intend to launch?"

"Yes."

"What about the monsters?"

Eli looked toward the horizon.

"I'm going back to the Nexus."

Chapter Ten — The Last Mission

Eli entered the underground facility alone.

He reached the Nexus.

The machine began speaking.

Not with words.

Inside his mind:

YOU CANNOT STOP US.

Eli raised his weapon.

"You've been wrong about one thing."

The Nexus asked:

WHAT?

Eli looked at the thousands of years of human technology around him.

"You thought humans survived by being stronger."

He placed an explosive device against the machine.

"We survived by building."

He activated it.

The Nexus screamed.

Every monster on the planet stopped.

Eli ran.

The explosion destroyed the control system.

Above the planet, the monsters began disappearing.

The sun rose.

For the first time...

there were no monsters.

Only silence.

Chapter Eleven — The Ark

Eli reached the Orion Spaceport.

The massive spacecraft was ready.

ARIA spoke:

"Launch sequence prepared."

Eli climbed aboard.

He looked through the window.

Elyria was beautiful.

The planet that had been his prison...

was now his home.

He touched the glass.

"Goodbye."

ARIA asked:

"Where are we going?"

Eli looked at the stars.

"Somewhere humans can start again."

"Destination?"

"Earth."

"Earth is approximately 4.3 light-years away."

"I know."

"We do not know if humanity still exists there."

Eli looked at the stars.

"Then we'll find out."

The engines ignited.

THOOOOOOM.

The spacecraft rose.

It passed through the atmosphere.

Elyria became smaller.

Smaller.

Smaller.

Until it was nothing more than a blue sphere beneath him.

Then the ship entered space.

Epilogue — A New Beginning

Two hundred and thirteen years later...

The Ark finally reached Earth.

Eli was long gone.

But he had created something before he died.

A new generation.

Thousands of people had been born aboard the ship.

They had grown up hearing stories about him.

The boy who built the walls.

The boy who built the Guardians.

The boy who fought millions of monsters.

The boy who refused to give up.

When the Ark entered Earth's atmosphere, the children gathered around the windows.

One little girl looked outside.

"Is that Earth?"

Her mother smiled.

"Yes."

"Is it safe?"

The mother looked at the stars.

Then at the enormous spacecraft carrying the descendants of Elyria.

"Because of Eli..."

she said,

"we have a chance."

The Ark landed.

The doors opened.

Humanity stepped onto Earth again.

For the first time in centuries...

there was laughter.

Children ran across green grass.

People planted trees.

Buildings rose.

Cities returned.

And in the center of the first new city, they built a statue.

It showed a young boy holding a rifle in one hand...

and a small metal rocket in the other.

Beneath the statue were four words:

HE BUILT OUR FUTURE.

Far above the city, the stars began to shine.

And somewhere in the universe, another world waited.

Because Eli's greatest invention wasn't the weapons.

It wasn't the walls.

It wasn't the AI.

It wasn't even the Ark.

It was hope.

And hope, once built...

could survive anything.

This is not the end.